Today, I focused on the "Write" side of SQL. Here are the core commands I practiced:

INSERT INTO: Adding new records to my tables.

UPDATE: Modifying existing data (and remembering the golden rule: Always use a WHERE clause!).

DELETE: Removing specific rows from the database.

ALTER TABLE: Changing the schema by adding or dropping columns.

💻 Code Snippets
SQL
-- 1. Inserting a new record
INSERT INTO Employees (name, role, salary) 
VALUES ('Jane Doe', 'Software Engineer', 85000);

-- 2. Updating data (Safety first: filtering by ID)
UPDATE Employees 
SET salary = 90000 
WHERE employee_id = 1;

-- 3. Adding a new column to an existing table
ALTER TABLE Employees 
ADD COLUMN department VARCHAR(50);
🧠 Key Takeaway
The biggest lesson today was understanding Constraints. I learned how NOT NULL, UNIQUE, and PRIMARY KEY act as the "guardrails" of a database to ensure data integrity.
